  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Wednesday)\nIssued at 112 PM CDT THU SEP 24 2026\n\nToday, the area will be between a low pressure to the northeast\nand a developing high over the MS River Valley. Flow will be from\nthe north, with dry and cool air advecting into the state. The\nlower levels will be the last to see this dry air, so expect\nclouds to hang around through much of the evening with a shallow\nsaturated layer lingering. Any drizzle over the state should also\nslowly weaken and dry, with dry conditions expected by late\nafternoon and evening. Temperatures tonight will be a solid\n10 degrees colder than they were this morning. As the northerly\nflow continues through the weekend, continuing to advect cool and\ndry air, the low temperatures will reach their lowest Saturday\nmorning with mid 50s in the northeast and lower 60s in the\nsouthwest.\n\nFriday through Sunday will be dry and cool, and the heat indices\nwill be at the actual temperature. By Monday, models start to\ndiverge. There has been a slower trend to the models so far with\nthe next frontal boundary. Next week, a weak low pressure system\nand trough will move west to east, bringing our next chance of\nrain. With models slowing down the eastern progression of this\nfeature, would expect rain to move into the state sometime around\nmid week, if not slightly later.\n\nLow and mid level flow will transition to out of the south during\nthe day on Monday ahead of this next system, and remain from the\nsouth for several days. This will allow for a gradual warming\ntrend to set up, with moisture slowly advecting back into the\nstate. Temperatures shouldn't be as high as they were, but highs\nreaching into the low 90s is likely by Tuesday and remaining\nuntil the rain moves in.\n\n24",
